<p>A federal judge in Florida dismissed President Donald Trump’s $10 billion defamation lawsuit against The Wall Street Journal and Rupert Murdoch over a report on Trump’s ties to Jeffrey Epstein. The judge ruled that the complaint did not plausibly show the article was published with actual malice, the standard US defamation law requires public figures to meet. Judge Darrin Gayles gave Trump until April 27 to file an amended complaint, so the case could continue in revised form rather than ending outright. The lawsuit centered on a Wall Street Journal article that said Trump had sent Epstein a birthday letter in 2003 as part of an album of messages for Epstein’s 50th birthday. Trump said the report was false and harmed his reputation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/5CiBOyRTnByDknI8SXUO8J-nzn9lnk2pXrccPDhGGgUhj0EEnSS3AmX39p8wqsfp5oSnJJNp6E1VOWbOTUvJ_OM4Vc5MtR4iM7QDLmiEuQzkM_HbYIjOn8wq8BdF-WusJG9-Fsu1Q6FxKkQMTKx61zFt3MY4eio0ZWTIEvev9IbuBIIRFBYqKcO_FDb14x81_3u-jASlH4CWHl_a0uATG7HdnnlAu3vbHOQTdWt57c3FdUdf02b7H_lnKYL-lRqT8ZMCvCSOHylZNvCxLA' alt='President Donald Trump speaks to reporters at Joint Base Andrews in Maryland on April 12, 2026, after returning from Miami.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Trump team vows to continue: A spokesperson said the President would comply with the ruling and file a revised lawsuit against the Journal and the other defendants.</li><li>The order ran 17 pages.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>The Hindu framed the dismissal as another setback for the administration’s efforts to manage fallout from the Epstein files and for Trump’s use of lawsuits against critical coverage. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/judge-dismisses-trumps-10-billion-lawsuit-against-wsj-murdoch-over-reporting-on-ties-to-epstein/article70858303.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3349958/judge-dismisses-trumps-us10-billion-wall-street-journal-